<BaseLayout title="CV22 (Tiantu) — Celestial Chimney Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat CV22 (Tiantu) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "CV22 (Tiantu)", href: "/points/cv22-tiantu-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">CV22 (Tiantu) — Celestial Chimney</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">CV22, known as Tiantu or Celestial Chimney, sits in the hollow above the breastbone. It is a first-line point for coughs, sore throats, hoarseness, and the urge to clear the throat — and because it sits close to the windpipe, it must be pressed very lightly and briefly.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Conception Vessel (CV / Ren)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Center of the hollow above the breastbone (suprasternal notch)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Cough, sore throat, hoarseness, throat clearing</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Very easy to find — but requires the lightest touch of any point on the site</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">10–20 seconds, very light</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">A few short sessions daily, as needed</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LU7 (Lieque)</a>, <a href="/points/cv17-shanzhong-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV17 (Shanzhong)</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Thyroid problems, recent throat or neck surgery, or trouble swallowing — and never press deeply (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why CV22 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">CV22 is a point on the Conception Vessel, the meridian that runs down the midline of the front of the body and is classically associated with the throat, chest, and breathing. Its name — Celestial Chimney — captures its position at the very top of the windpipe, in the dip where the collarbones meet just above the breastbone. Because it sits so close to the airway, traditional practice uses it for anything that blocks or irritates the throat: a dry tickle, a hoarse voice, a stubborn cough, or a feeling that something needs to be cleared.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The point is remarkably easy to reach — you can press it at a desk, in a car, or before giving a presentation — and it responds to very light touch. Speakers, singers, and anyone with a throat-clearing habit often find that a few seconds of gentle pressure settles the reflex. Because of the nearby windpipe, the guidance here is more about gentleness than force: a light fingertip hold rather than deep pressure is the correct technique, and short sessions are safer and often just as effective.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/cv22-tiantu-location.webp" alt="CV22 acupressure point location in the hollow of the throat" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="455" height="279" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">CV22 sits in the suprasternal notch — the soft, V-shaped hollow at the base of the front of the throat, between the inner ends of the two collarbones and directly above the breastbone. To find it, tip your head back slightly and run a finger down the front of your neck; you will feel the notch where the neck meets the chest. The point is at the center of that hollow. It is not on the Adam's apple (which sits higher) and not on the windpipe itself — it is the dip just above the breastbone.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) tip the head back and run a finger down the front of the neck — the point is the soft V-shaped dip just above the breastbone, not the Adam's apple; (2) it is in the exact midline, between the inner ends of the collarbones; (3) pressing it gently produces a slight fullness or tickle sensation — if you feel pressure on the windpipe itself, you are too deep or too high.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use CV22 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• A dry, tickly cough is building</li>
          <li>• Your throat feels scratchy or sore</li>
          <li>• Your voice is hoarse or tired after speaking, singing, or shouting</li>
          <li>• You keep needing to clear your throat</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You have a thyroid condition or swelling at the front of the neck</li>
          <li>• You recently had throat, thyroid, or neck surgery</li>
          <li>• Swallowing is painful or difficult, or you have a high fever</li>
          <li>• There is skin irritation, a rash, or swelling over the point</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: CV22 is a point for gentle throat comfort — never deep pressure — and a painful or persistent sore throat needs a clinician rather than more pressing.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Cough:</strong> CV22 is traditionally used to soothe coughing and the urge to cough from a tickly throat.</li>
      <li><strong>Sore throat:</strong> It may help ease throat discomfort and a dry, scratchy sensation.</li>
      <li><strong>Hoarseness and voice strain:</strong> It is a favorite point for singers and speakers with a tired or weak voice.</li>
      <li><strong>Difficulty swallowing:</strong> It is used for the sensation of a lump or mild discomfort when swallowing.</li>
      <li><strong>Throat clearing:</strong> It helps settle a nervous or habitual need to clear the throat.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For a complete step-by-step routine, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-anxiety" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">anxiety acupressure guide</a>.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage CV22</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">This point is close to the windpipe, so keep the touch light and the session short — a gentle fingertip hold of 10–20 seconds is plenty. Never press downward or backward into the throat.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Tilt your head back slightly to open the hollow above the breastbone.</li>
      <li>Place the pad of your index or middle finger in the center of the notch.</li>
      <li>Apply very light pressure straight onto the pad of the finger — not down toward the windpipe.</li>
      <li>Hold for 10–20 seconds while breathing slowly, then release.</li>
      <li>Repeat a few times a day as needed, keeping every session short and gentle.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing downward or backward toward the windpipe, or holding for too long. CV22 sits near the airway, so the correct technique is a light touch on the pad of the finger, held for seconds rather than minutes — if it feels uncomfortable at all, stop.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine CV22 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A throat routine works better than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, keeping CV22 holds short and gentle.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Dry tickly cough</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV22 → <a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LU7</a> → <a href="/points/cv17-shanzhong-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV17</a>, light holds, plus warmth and fluids</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Sore throat / first sign of a cold</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV22 → LU7 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, gentle 10–20s holds, repeated through the day</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Hoarse or tired voice</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV22 → CV17, short gentle holds, with voice rest and sips of warm water</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stuffy nose with a cough</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV22 → <a href="/points/li20-yingxiang-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI20</a> → LU7</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">CV22 lives on the <a href="/body/chest-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">chest acupressure points</a> body guide. For a fuller cold-care program, the <a href="/body/head-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">head acupressure points</a> guide adds GB20 and LI20 to round out the routine — and review the evidence on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">does acupressure work?</a> page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With a light 10–20 second hold, the throat often feels a little calmer within moments. The urge to clear may briefly strengthen before settling — that is normal. Do not expect a single press to stop a cold in its tracks.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">A few short, gentle sessions a day can ease a tickly cough and hoarseness as part of your usual cold or voice care. Keep every session brief — with CV22, gentleness and brevity are the technique, not a compromise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">A cough or sore throat that lasts more than a week or two, or that is getting worse, needs a clinician. CV22 is comfort care, not a treatment for respiratory or throat conditions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for respiratory and pain conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Studies looking at points around the neck and chest have reported that gentle pressure may help ease throat discomfort, coughing, and congestion, but the trials are small and use varied techniques, so the evidence is not conclusive. Our overview of the <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">current evidence for acupressure</a> explains what the research can and cannot tell us.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">The direction of the evidence is positive: acupressure appears to be a low-risk, low-cost self-care option that some people find genuinely helpful for respiratory symptoms and voice fatigue. Because CV22 sits close to the airway, the most important lesson from the research is caution — gentle, short stimulation is the safe approach, and persistent throat problems, pain on swallowing, or a worsening cough should always be checked by a doctor.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["song-2015"]} note="The review above assessed self-acupressure across respiratory complaints. It is not a study of CV22 in isolation, but CV22 is the point most commonly used for cough and throat relief." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Use only very light, brief pressure — never press downward or backward toward the windpipe.</li>
      <li>Avoid the point after recent throat, thyroid, or neck surgery, or if you have a known neck condition.</li>
      <li>Avoid the point if you have a thyroid condition affecting the front of the neck.</li>
      <li>Skip it if there is swelling, pain, or skin irritation at the front of the neck, or if swallowing is difficult.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel a gag reflex, difficulty breathing, or any discomfort; seek medical care for a painful or persistently sore throat.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Difficulty breathing or swallowing</li>
        <li>A high fever with a sore throat</li>
        <li>A sore throat that lasts more than a few days or keeps getting worse</li>
        <li>A visible lump or swelling in the neck</li>
        <li>Hoarseness lasting more than 2–3 weeks (see a doctor or ENT specialist)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is CV22 safe to press?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes, as long as the touch is very light and brief — 10–20 seconds with the pad of a fingertip. It sits near the windpipe, so never press downward or backward into the throat, and stop if you feel any discomfort or a gag reflex.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can CV22 help my voice or throat clearing habit?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">CV22 is traditionally used for hoarseness, a tired voice, and the urge to clear the throat. A few short, gentle holds when your throat feels scratchy can be a useful alternative to repeated throat clearing, which itself irritates the throat further.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press CV22?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Keep holds short — 10–20 seconds — and repeat a few times a day as needed. Unlike most acupressure points, CV22 does not respond better to long or deep pressure; gentle and brief is the correct approach here.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press CV22 before a talk or meeting?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. A light 10–20 second hold in the hollow above the breastbone is quick and looks like a natural gesture, so it works before speaking, singing, or a long phone call. Breathe slowly while you hold, and avoid pressing if your throat feels painful or swollen.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor if a sore throat lasts more than a few days, if swallowing becomes painful or difficult, if you have a high fever, or if a cough keeps getting worse. Hoarseness lasting more than 2–3 weeks, or a lump in the neck, also needs a review. Acupressure is a comfort measure, not a treatment for serious throat or airway conditions.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to All Points</a>
    </div>
  </article>
